      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "With both coefficients free, every nonconstant positive sample has a unique finite MLE. Constant data have unbounded likelihood. Holding $\\alpha$ gives the unique endpoint estimate $b=\\min_i x_i$, even for constant data. Holding $b\\le\\min_i x_i$ gives a unique finite shape estimate unless every observation equals $b$, when the likelihood is unbounded. A held endpoint above an observation gives zero likelihood. Holding both leaves likelihood evaluation.",
      "reason": "For fixed positive $\\alpha$, the log-likelihood increases strictly in $b$ on $0<b\\le\\min_i x_i$. With $b$ fixed, its shape-dependent part is $n\\log\\alpha-\\alpha\\sum_i\\log(x_i/b)$. It is strictly concave and has maximizer $n/\\sum_i\\log(x_i/b)$ when that sum is positive; otherwise it increases without bound as $\\alpha\\to\\infty$."
